An ID Token is strictly for the Client application to know who logged in (like a digital driver's license). An Access Token is for the API (like a hotel room keycard). Even better: because this is a Machine-to-Machine (ClientCredentials) flow with no human user, Duende doesn't even issue an ID Token! (And on Step 1, the daemon calls POST /connect/token directly, because GET /connect/authorize is only for interactive browser logins).
Duende signs the JWT with its Private Key, InventoryApi only needs to download Duende's Public Key once from /.well-known/openid-configuration/jwks and cache it in memory. Every incoming request is verified locally in microseconds using CPU math—zero network calls to Duende required.



sequenceDiagram
    
    participant Daemon as Warehouse Daemon (Client)
    participant Duende as Duende IdentityServer (STS)
    participant API as Inventory API (Resource Server)

    Note over API,Duende: On Startup (or cached periodically):<br/>API fetches Discovery & Public Keys (/.well-known/openid-configuration/jwks)
    Daemon->>Duende: POST /connect/token (grant_type=client_credentials, client_id, client_secret, scope=inventory.read)
    Duende-->>Daemon: 200 OK (Returns Access_Token only)
    Daemon->>API: GET /api/stock (Authorization: Bearer <Access_Token>)
    Note over API: Validates JWT signature, 'iss', 'aud', 'exp', and 'typ'<br/>locally in memory using cached Public Key
    API-->>Daemon: 200 OK (Stock JSON)



RFC 9068
Both ID Tokens and Access Tokens are signed by the exact same Duende private key. However:

Duende stamps ID Tokens with "typ": "JWT".

Duende stamps Access Tokens with "typ": "at+jwt".

By setting options.TokenValidationParameters.ValidTypes = ["at+jwt"], JwtBearerMiddleware inspects the header first and immediately rejects any ID Token someone tries to sneak into an Authorization: Bearer header—even though its cryptographic signature is completely valid.